:"danger","note":"Documented dangerous interaction on PsychonautWiki."},{"substance":"Ketamine","status":"Dangerous","tone":"danger","note":"Documented dangerous interaction on PsychonautWiki."},{"substance":"MXE","status":"Dangerous","tone":"danger","note":"Documented dangerous interaction on PsychonautWiki."},{"substance":"Tramadol","status":"Dangerous","tone":"danger","note":"Documented dangerous interaction on PsychonautWiki."},{"substance":"MAOIs","status":"Caution","tone":"warning","note":"Uncertain or unpredictable interaction on PsychonautWiki."},{"substance":"Nitrous","status":"Caution","tone":"warning","note":"Uncertain or unpredictable interaction on PsychonautWiki."},{"substance":"PCP","status":"Caution","tone":"warning","note":"Uncertain or unpredictable interaction on PsychonautWiki."},{"substance":"Stimulants","status":"Caution","tone":"warning","note":"Uncertain or unpredictable interaction on PsychonautWiki."}],"citations":[],"helpfulLinks":[{"title":"PsychonautWiki","description":"Pharmacology, subjective effects, and dosage reference","url":"https://psychonautwiki.org/wiki/U-47700"},{"title":"TripSit Factsheet","description":"Rapid harm-reduction summary and combination data","url":"https://drugs.tripsit.me/u-47700"},{"title":"Erowid Experience Vaults","description":"First-person experience reports and archives","url":"https://www.erowid.org/experiences/subs/exp_U47700.shtml"},{"title":"Wikipedia","description":"Encyclopedia article","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U-47700"},{"title":"Wikidata","description":"Structured data on U-47700","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q83129025"}],"wikidata":"Q83129025","pharmacology":{"text":"U-47700 is an agonist of the μ-opioid receptor (Ki 11.1 ± 0.4 nM) and possesses significantly lower affinity for the κ-opioid receptor (Ki = 287 ± 24 nM) and δ-opioid receptor (Ki = 1220 ± 82 nM). U-47700 is approximately 10-fold more potent than morphine in rats, although the binding of U-47700 is 2–4 times weaker than morphine at all three opioid receptors.","source":"Wikipedia","page":"U-47700","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U-47700#Pharmacology"},"uses":{"text":"U-47700, also known as U4, pink heroin, pinky, and pink, is an opioid analgesic drug developed by a team at Upjohn in the 1970s which has around 7.5 times the potency of morphine in animal models.","source":"Wikipedia","page":"U-47700","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U-47700"},"sideEffects":{"text":"U-47700 has never been studied in humans, but it would be expected to produce effects similar to those of other potent opioid agonists, including strong analgesia, sedation, euphoria, constipation, itching and respiratory depression which could be harmful or fatal. Tachycardia was another side effect encountered with U-47700 use. Tolerance and dependence would be expected to develop.","source":"Wikipedia","page":"U-47700","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U-47700#Side_effects"}}